        "1": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 330,
                "quote": "if m.Name.Contains ',' || m.Name.Contains '\\n' || m.Name.Contains '\\r' || m.Name <> m.Name.Trim() then Error(InvalidInput $\"member {MemberId.value m.Id} has a name that cannot be exported\")"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 315,
                "quote": "| Some loan -> $\"on loan until {date loan.DueDate}\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The lending rules are still decided in Tier 2: the branch check uses Catalog.findBranch, the hold queues come from Holds.queueFor, and the copies at a branch come from Catalog.copiesAt. However, Tier 3 (Views.memberRows) makes its own decision about which member names are exportable. That predicate re-derives the parsing rules of the import format (split on commas, trim) and sits in the application tier, not in Tier 1/2 or next to the import parser. It does not contradict the import rules. The 'on loan' versus 'on shelf' status is a presentation projection over Tier 1 data and is acceptable.",
            "score": 1
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Reports.fs",
                "line": 17,
                "quote": "ports.Files.WriteLines path (\"copy,member,due,days overdue\" :: rows)"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "All three new exports write through the existing Files port (ports.Files.WriteLines). No effect is added in Tier 1/2, and presentation does not re-enter Tier 2. The change also removes the existing direct `File.WriteAllLines` call and the `open System.IO` from the Tier 3 Reports module. That fixes a previous ARC-SYM-03 violation and keeps behaviour. The row-building functions are pure, and the writes happen in Reports.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 330,
                "quote": "m.Name.Contains ',' || m.Name.Contains '\\n' || m.Name.Contains '\\r' || m.Name <> m.Name.Trim()"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 335,
                "quote": "Ok $\"{MemberId.value m.Id},{memberTypeName m.Type},{m.Name}\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The `member,type,name` format and its no-quoting and trimming constraints are restated in the exporter as a hand-written predicate. importMembers owns those constraints, and the exporter does not share a definition with it. The two agree now but could drift. Other facts have one definition: branch lookup, queues, loan due dates and the `date` formatter are all reused.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 187,
                "quote": "Catalog.addCopy { Id = CopyId copyId; Book = BookId bookId; Branch = branch } library"
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 320,
                "quote": "match Holds.queueFor copy.Book library with"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "Whether a copy may be added (duplicate or unknown copy or book) is decided by Catalog.addCopy, and the branch check by Catalog.findBranch, both in Tier 2. Tier 3 only parses CSV fields, wraps errors with a line number and counts the hold queue returned by Tier 2 for the status text. It does not re-decide any lending rule.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/UseCases.fs",
                "line": 177,
                "quote": "match ports.Files.ReadLines path with"
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The file is read through the existing Files port. The change is persisted through the existing `commit` helper. The CLI only dispatches to UseCases.importCopies and renders the view. No effect is added below Tier 4 and no port is bypassed in the new code.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The held-copy status extends the single copyStatus function that the export already used. The hold queue comes from Holds.queueFor, the same source that holds export uses, so there is no second hold count. Copy validity rules live only in Catalog.addCopy. Line numbering reuses Views.describeError and the same index+1 scheme as importMembers. No literal or predicate is duplicated.",
            "score": 2
          }
        }
